                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Lost girl's [diary] is a short horror visual novel that takes place in a snowy hotel during Christmas. I follow a group of girls who are spending their vacation away from the city when their plans for fortune telling turn into a night of telling scary stories. Their boyfriends arrive and interrupt everything, but the evening quickly becomes more uncomfortable as the stories and personal problems between the characters start to come together. It is a very short game, with a playtime of around an hour, so I see it more as a small horror story than a long visual novel.\n\nWhat I like most is the setting. A remote hotel covered in snow is already a good place for a horror story, and having the characters trapped together during Christmas gives the whole thing a slightly uneasy feeling. The game also gives the main girls their own personalities instead of making them feel completely interchangeable. Sasha is responsible and caring, Vera is more aggressive and willing to use violence, and the characters have their own histories and problems that come out as the story progresses. I think that helps the short runtime because I do not need a huge amount of exposition to understand that there is more going on between these people.\n\nThe actual horror is probably the weakest part for me. The game describes its stories as being the sort of horror that would only really scare children, and I think that is a fair way of looking at it. There are some creepy moments and the atmosphere does a decent job of making the hotel feel uncomfortable, but I never found it particularly frightening. I think it works better when I treat it as a dark little Christmas story with horror elements rather than expecting something genuinely terrifying. That is not necessarily a bad thing, but it does limit how much impact some of the supposedly scary moments have.\n\nI also think the short length works both ways. On one hand, I appreciate that the game gets to the point and does not stretch a simple story into something much longer than it needs to be. I can finish it in roughly an hour and walk away having experienced the whole thing. On the other hand, I would have liked more time with the characters and their relationships because there are some interesting details in their backgrounds that could have been explored further. The game has the foundations for a more involved story, but it mostly chooses to keep things brief.\n\nThe visual novel presentation is simple, but I think it suits the project. The snowy hotel gives the backgrounds a consistent atmosphere, while the character focused scenes keep most of my attention on the conversations and story. There are also Steam achievements, which gives me a small reason to replay the game after finishing it. I would not say the presentation is especially impressive, but it does what it needs to do without getting in the way of the story.\n\nMy biggest issue is that I wanted more from the story. The basic setup is interesting, and the characters have enough personality to make me curious about them, but the limited runtime means there is not much room for the mystery or horror to develop. Some of the events feel more like a quick sequence of ideas than a fully developed horror story. I also think the game could have done more with the hotel itself because the isolated location is easily one of the strongest parts of the setup.\n\nOverall, Lost girl's [diary] is a decent short visual novel that I think works best when I go into it expecting a quick horror story rather than a deep or genuinely scary experience. I like the snowy Christmas setting, the character personalities and the simple visual novel format, while the weak scares, limited development and very short length stop it from reaching a higher score for me. It is free, takes around an hour to finish and has enough atmosphere to make the short trip worthwhile, but there is not quite enough here for me to consider it one of the stronger horror visual novels I have played.\n\nRating: 6.8/10\n",
